What is fcntl() ?

	fcntl() is a system call used to manipulate file desciptors 

	Using fcntl(), we can : 

		* Duplicate file descriptors 
		* Get/set file descriptor flags 
		* Get/set file status flags 
		* Perform file locking 


Header files 
	
	#include <unistd.h>
	#include <fcntl.h>
	

Function prototype 

	int fcntl(int fd, int cmd, ... /* arg */);

	fd  -> file descriptor 
	cmd -> operation to perform 
	arg -> optional argument (depends on cmd)

	Whether arg is required depends on cmd 


Purpose of fcnt()

	The main aim of fcntl() is file descriptor control and file locking/unlocking 


F_DUPFD - Duplicate file descriptor 

	find the lowest available file desciptor >= arg and makes it a duplicate of fd

	newfd = fcntl(fd, F_DUPFD, arg);

	Example : 

		fd1 = fcntl(fd, F_DUPFD, 10);
		
		Output 

			fd1 = 11 

			Explanation 
			
				OS searches from fd 10 
				First fee fd found = 11
				11 becomes a copy of fd 

			Difference from dup() 

				dup() -> lowest free fd 
				F_DUPFD -> lowest free fd starting from arg 

F_GETFD - Get FD flags 

	flags = fcntl(fd, F_GETFD);

		Reads file descriptor flags
		arg is ignored 

	Example flag 

		FD_CLOEXEC

			close this file desciptor automatically when exec() is called. 


F_SETFD - Set FD flags 

	fcntl(fd, F_SETFD, flags);

		Sets file descriptor flags 
		
	Example 

		fcntl (fd, F_SETFD, FD_CLOEXEC);

F_GETFL - Get File Status Flags 

	flags = fcntl(fd, F_GETFL);
	
	Returns : 

		Accesss mode (O_RDONLY, O_WRONLY, O_RDWR)
		Status flags (O_APPEND, O_NONBLOCK, etc)

		arg is ignored 


F_SETFL - Set File Status Flags

	fcntl(fd, F_SETFL, O_NONBLOCK);

	Used to set flags like 

		O_NONBLOCK
		O_APPEND
		
	Access modes and creation flags are ignored 
	
		O_RDONLY, O_WRONLY, O_CREAT, etc cannot be changed 


